TI  - Omega-3 index is directly associated with a healthy red blood cell distribution width
T2  - PROSTAGLANDINS LEUKOTRIENES AND ESSENTIAL FATTY ACIDS
AB  - Low red blood cell (RBC) membrane content of EPA and DHA, i.e., the omega-3 index (O3I), and elevated RBC distribution width (RDW) are risk factors for all-cause mortality. O3I and RDW are related with membrane fluidity and deformability. Our objective was to determine if there is a relationship between O3I and RDW in healthy adults. Subjects without inflammation or anemia, and with values for O3I, RDW, high-sensitivity Creactive protein (CRP), body mass index (BMI), age and sex were identified (n = 25,485) from a clinical laboratory dataset of 0.00066) being especially steep in females with O3I <= 5.6%. In healthy adults of both sexes, the data suggested that an O3I of 5.6% may help maintain normal RBC structural and functional integrity.
