AU  - Sellmayer, A
TI  - n-3 Polyunsaturated fatty acids and their effect on cardiac mortality
T2  - CLINICAL NUTRITION
AB  - Cardiac mortality is mainly caused by fatal cardiac arrhythmias and acute or chronic pump failure. Epidemiological studies and four intervention trials with n-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) clearly show that n-3 PUFA consistently reduce cardiac mortality but not cardiac morbidity, specifically myocardial infarction, indicating that n-3 PUFA may be protective through anti-arrhythmic effects. In support of this notion, in vitro studies in neonatal tat cardiac myocytes as well as animal studies reveal that n-3 PUFA protect from potentially fatal cardiac arrhythmias by reducing cardiac myocyte excitability. Preliminary trials in man further indicate that n-3 PUFA may have anti-arrhythmic effects in humans, although the definite proof for their protection from fatal arrhythmias in high risk patients is still missing. Increased consumption of n-3 PUFA is now recommended by the American Heart Association. Given that further evidence of the anti-arrhythmic effects of n-3 PUFA will accumulate, a way to quickly and safely administer n-3 PUFA to high risk patients such as those suffering from acute myocardial infarction warrants further investigation. (C) 2002, Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.
