AU  - Nayebifar, SH
AU  - Ghasemi, E
AU  - Karimipour, S
TI  - Effect of high-intensity interval training and omega-3 supplementation on liver enzymes and lipid profile of young men
T2  - SCIENCE & SPORTS
AB  - Objectives. It has always been of great importance to measure hepatic enzymes following exercise in order to prevent liver disease. The aim of this study was to determine the effect of 6 weeks of severe periodic exercise with omega-3 supplementation on serum liver enzymes and lipid profile of inactive men.
   Patients and methods. In this quasi-experimental study, 32 young men aged between 20 and 30 years were selected via selective and targeted sampling method and were randomly assigned into four equal groups (n=8 per group) including omega-3, training-placebo, training-omega 3, and control. Training groups performed severe periodic exercises at 90% max heart rate for 6 weeks, and supplemental groups consumed two 1000 mg tablets of omega-3 on a daily basis. Blood samples were taken from the participants 24 hours before and 48 hours after the intervention in fasting state. Data were analyzed by means of Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, Two ways ANOVA, and LSD post hoc test. The significance level was considered at P < 0.05.
   Results. After 6 weeks of exercise and omega-3 supplementation, there were significant differences between the groups in terms of TG (P=0.02), ALT (P=0.02), AST (P=0.01), weight (P=0.01), and fat percentage (P= 0.01), while there was no significant difference in the TC, ALP, and WC levels (P > 0.05). Also, HDL (P= 0.01) and LDL (P=0.04) showed significantly increased and decreased levels in the excercise-omega-3 group.
   Conclusion. It seems that omega-3 consumption along with severe interval exercise is an effective factor in preventing from and improving cardiovascular risk factors and fatty liver in young inactive men since the regimen increases lipid metabolism due to the desirable effects on liver enzymes, lipid profile, and body composition. (C) 2019 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.
