TI  - (omega)3-unsaturated fatty acids in the treatment of mesangioproliferative glomerulonephritis?
T2  - NIEREN-UND HOCHDRUCKKRANKHEITEN
AB  - IgA nephropathy is recognized as the most common histologically verified primary glomerular disorder. Chronic renal failure occurs in approximately 13 - 20% of patients 10 years after diagnosis, and in as many as 25 - 34% after 20 years. The pathogenesis of IgA nephropathy is still purely understood. Overproduction of nephritogenic IgA or IgA immune complexes of either mucosal or non-mucosal origin, may play a major role. In addition impairment of clearance of circulating IgA immune complexes is also important. The deposition of these immune complexes in the mesangium provokes several mediator mechanisms which progressively damage the glomeruli by the action of complement, cytokines, growth factors, or prostaglandin. Most therapeutic regimes have not proved effective in controlled trials as approaches to prevent the formation of IgA immune complex or removing them, depressing the immune system or to reduce the formation or action of the mediators involved. The effect of fish oil ((omega)3-polyunsaturated fatty acids) has been shown in five studies, three small, well-designed prospective studies failed to produce a beneficial effect of this treatment. The results of Hamazaki and more recently of Donadio er al. are more promising. During the treatment period and follow-up lasting three pears on an average, the primary endpoint and the death or end-stage renal failure occurred less in the fish-oil group as compared to the placebo group. These results are promising and do not entail the risks associated with corticosteroids and immunosuppressive drugs, but the long-term effect has to be proved in future long-term controlled studies.
